the u.s and south korean militaries have  together launched eight ballistic  missiles into the sea on monday a  response to north korean launches a day  earlier it's the second time in recent  months that the u.s and its allies south  korea have jointly responded to similar  moves from north korea many see it as  representing a hardening of seoul's  approach to pyongyang  a fiery early morning display that took  all of ten minutes  its effects were meant to extend well  into the future  the launches of seven south korean  missiles and one american missile in a  live fire exercise  a tit-for-tat reply to north korea's  launching of eight missiles a day  earlier  [Music]  the response came as south korea and its  hawkish new president marked memorial  day commemorations  yoon sukul saying more than ever global  powers must work to contain north korea  north korea's nuclear and missile  threats are getting sophisticated it  fired various ballistic missiles  yesterday  its programs are reaching the level that  threaten not only the peace of the  korean peninsula but also northeast asia  and the world  the world has almost grown accustomed to  seeing this type of weapons  demonstration from north korea  indeed in 2022 alone north korea has  launched 18 rounds of missile tests  including intercontinental ballistic  missiles  military intelligence suggests president  kim jong-un is pushing for north korea's  first nuclear test in nearly five years  north korea has repeatedly launched  ballistic missiles including new icbms  at a high frequency since the beginning  of this year  a series of north korean actions  threaten the peace and stability of the  region and the international community  they cannot be tolerated  the north's previous launches were last  month as u.s president joe biden wrapped  up a trip to south korea and japan  when biden reaffirmed the u.s commitment  to defending both allies  and kim reaffirmed his desire for north  korea to become a nuclear power  and joining me now for more from seoul  is reuters senior correspondent josh  smith josh joint u.s and south korean  missile launchers in response to north  korea does this somehow symbolize the  new south korean policy towards its  northern neighbor  it is certainly a return uh in some ways  to uh a new old policy uh one that we  last uh saw in significant uh levels  back in 2017 during the days of uh the  fire and fury threats between kim  jong-un and former u.s president uh  donald trump um this is not the first  time they've done these joint drills uh  this year they also rolled out joint uh  missile drills uh late last month uh  when north korea tested an icbm the day  after u.s president joe biden left the  region  after his first talks in the region but  as you mentioned uh south korean  president yun sukyol has promised to  make this more of a regular occurrence  in response to to north korean missile  launches and given that north korea  appears uh intent on  increasing uh the number of missiles  that it tests i think it's a fair bet to  say that we'll see more of these from  the united states and south korea as  well  talk to us a bit about those north  korean missile launches should they be  viewed as missile tests or a sign of  diplomacy north korea style  so there are uh certainly multiple  reasons why north korea would test  missiles including domestic messaging as  well as basically uh you know just  developing the military technology that  it wants uh at the same time there is  undeniably a geopolitical message beyond  that uh the timing of these latest  missiles for example came right after uh  south korea and the united states held  the first joint naval drill involving an  american aircraft carrier uh in a number  of years and so it's hard to see that as  entirely a coincidence  at the same time north korea has been  battling uh what it says is its first  cobit outbreak and something that's  interesting that's happened during that  time is that they've stopped publicizing  or releasing any photos of their missile  tests so we're basically solely  dependent on what countries like south  korea and japan report  so unlike some of their other tests  earlier this year these latest tests  we don't know a lot of details we don't  know exactly what they fired um although  they do appear to be short-range  missiles uh so not its biggest missiles  which some analysts say would suggest  perhaps a military drill practicing uh  rather than developing uh some kind of  new technology  i did want to talk to you about north  korea's covert outbreak i mean given  that they've announced they've admitted  that they have an outbreak it also  appears to be that they don't have  enough vaccinations or any vaccinations  for that matter or enough medical  supplies to cope with this yet we are  seeing these missile launches i mean  what does that say about the country's  priorities  uh that is a big question and one that  for example uh you know  washington has uh not been hesitant to  point out um saying that you know if if  sanctions were really causing so many  problems why do they have the resources  to conduct these tests at the same time  i mean north korea has said that  these tests are its uh self-defense and  basically a a matter of life and death  for the country itself um and that's the  case that they've made to domestic  audiences before at the same time you  know as mentioned they haven't been  publicizing this including to folks at  home and uh analysts have said that may  point to them being a bit worried about  the imagery of them uh continuing to  shoot these off while not having the  resources necessarily to battle the  covet outbreak  and in the midst of all this there are  also reports that the north is preparing  for its first nuclear test since  september 2017 what are you hearing in  seoul  so the the last we heard uh was late  last month from south korean officials  who uh  went beyond kind of what we'd seen in in  satellite imagery of them preparing some  of the underground tunnels that they  previously used for the tests um to say  that they had actually um uh that north  korea had actually begun testing  components of a nuclear weapon  detonators and other things  which suggested that it was basically uh  any day now that we might see a new test  basically what officials here and  analysts have said is that  by all accounts and from everything that  they can tell all of the technical um  progress has been made toward resuming  tests it basically all just comes down  to when kim jong-un decides  to conduct a new test or not  we'll leave it there for the time being  thank you so much for joining us from  seoul josh smith
 

